{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Nitrogen Thieves of the Peruvian Shelf: Thioploca's Nitrate Elevator",
    "date": "2026-09-01",
    "lesson": "Off Peru, the sulfate-reducing sediment beneath the upwelling shelf is rich in sulfide but has no oxygen. Vacuolate sulfur bacteria like Thioploca and Thiomargarita solve this by acting as walking (or gliding) batteries: they store nitrate they absorb near the sediment-water interface and haul it down into the sulfidic mud in vacuoles, using it in place of oxygen to oxidize sulfide. This scenario models that vertical commute as a chemosynthetic band that must straddle the overlap between a nitrate-rich upper layer and a sulfide-rich lower layer. Too shallow and there's no sulfide to burn; too deep and the nitrate runs out. Diatom blooms rain down from the productive surface above, feeding an ordinary heterotroph community, while the chemolithotrophs occupy the redox knife-edge below.",
    "citation": "Fossing et al. 1995, Nature - Thioploca and nitrate storage in Peru shelf sediments",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Peru-Chile upwelling oxygen minimum zone shelf sediments, Thioploca/Thiomargarita nitrate-sulfide vertical migration"
  },
